instructions:
Implement the bot game-loop simulator in game-core/src/ai/sim.ts (NO classes, NO mutation) plus its 20-game soak test. This is M2-T05: the integration proof that the whole engine — setup, turn loop, explore, buying, death/revival — works end-to-end with REAL Premier card data and REAL bots, without illegal moves or crashes. All behavior below was verified against the running engine (the expected values are real observed results).

Imports (exact, and ONLY these):
```ts
import type { Action, GameState, GameSetup } from '../types';
import { setup, startTurn, checkWin } from '../engine/game';
import { applyAction } from '../engine/actions';
import { chooseBotAction, type BotDifficulty } from './bot';
import { PREMIER_CARDS, FIRST_TIMER_PILES } from '../data/cards';
import { makeRng, nextFloat } from '../util/rng';
```

STYLE RULES: strict TS with noUncheckedIndexedAccess. No `any`. No default exports. Never mutate inputs. Export exactly:

```ts
export interface SimConfig {
  readonly seed: number;
  /** Difficulty per seat, in seat order. Length must equal the player count. */
  readonly difficulties: readonly BotDifficulty[];
  /** Hard cap on applied actions; the game stops there if no winner yet. */
  readonly maxActions: number;
}

export interface SimResult {
  readonly state: GameState;
  readonly actionsApplied: number;
  /** 'gameOver' when checkWin fired; 'cap' when maxActions was hit first. */
  readonly ended: 'gameOver' | 'cap';
}
```

```ts
export function playBotGame(config: SimConfig): SimResult
```
Behavior: build `const players = config.difficulties.map((d, i) => ({ name: 'Bot' + i, controller: 'bot' as const, botLevel: (d === 'hard' ? 3 : d === 'normal' ? 2 : 1) }));` and `const setupConfig: GameSetup = { seed: config.seed, players };`. Then `return playBotGameFrom(setup(setupConfig, PREMIER_CARDS, FIRST_TIMER_PILES), config.difficulties, rngFromSeed(config.seed), config.maxActions);` where `rngFromSeed(seed)` is a local helper: `let rng = makeRng(seed); return () => { const r = nextFloat(rng); rng = r.rng; return r.value; };`.

```ts
export function playBotGameFrom(state: GameState, difficulties: readonly BotDifficulty[], rand: () => number, maxActions: number): SimResult
```
Behavior — the game loop (this is the heart of M2-T05):
1. `let current = state; let actions = 0;`
2. Loop while `actions < maxActions`:
   a. `const win = checkWin(current);` if `win.gameOver` break (ended = 'gameOver').
   b. `const seat = current.activeSeat;` — the active seat ALWAYS has legal moves (enumerate includes endTurn); look up `difficulties[seat]` with a fallback to 'easy' if undefined (defensive under noUncheckedIndexedAccess).
   c. `const action = chooseBotAction(current, seat, difficulty, rand);`
   d. `current = applyAction(current, action);` — throws if the action were illegal (it cannot be: chooseBotAction only returns validator-legal actions); let it propagate.
   e. `actions += 1;`
   f. `if (action.type === 'endTurn') current = startTurn(current);` — the engine's endTurn action advances the seat but does NOT reset pools or handle downed revival; startTurn does (and guards on phase). Calling it here is REQUIRED — without it the next player keeps spent pools and the game degenerates.
3. After the loop: `const final = checkWin(current);` — if `final.gameOver` and the loop exited via cap, still report ended = 'gameOver' (check the flag AFTER the loop too; ended = final.gameOver ? 'gameOver' : 'cap').
4. Return `{ state: current, actionsApplied: actions, ended }`.

Engineering comments to include: (a) the loop is deterministic for a fixed seed — same rand sequence, same everything; (b) applyAction throws on illegal actions by design, so the soak fails loudly on rules bugs — do not try/catch.

----------------- File 2: game-core/src/ai/sim.test.ts -----------------

Vitest (globals). Import the four fixtures/exports from sim + helpers. The soak: mixed difficulties per the plan (2 easy, 1 normal, 1 hard = 'clumsy human proxies' + 'policy bots'), REAL data, hard caps. Tests EXACTLY:

1. 'ten mixed games terminate without throwing' — for seed = 1..10 (plain for loop): `const r = playBotGame({ seed, difficulties: ['easy', 'easy', 'normal', 'hard'], maxActions: 400 });` assert `r.ended` is 'gameOver' or 'cap'; assert `r.actionsApplied > 0`; assert `Number.isFinite(r.state.turnNumber)`. (If any game throws, the test fails — that is the point.)
2. 'deterministic replay of an identical config' — const a = playBotGame({ seed: 7, difficulties: ['easy', 'easy', 'normal', 'hard'], maxActions: 400 }); const b = playBotGame({ seed: 7, difficulties: ['easy', 'easy', 'normal', 'hard'], maxActions: 400 }); expect(JSON.stringify(a.state)).toBe(JSON.stringify(b.state)); expect(a.ended).toBe(b.ended); expect(a.actionsApplied).toBe(b.actionsApplied).
3. 'cap games end with a live un-game-over board' — for seed = 2..4: r = playBotGame({ seed, difficulties: ['easy', 'easy', 'normal', 'hard'], maxActions: 200 }); if r.ended === 'cap': expect(r.actionsApplied).toBe(200); expect(checkWin(r.state).gameOver).toBe(false). (Some of these may actually hit gameOver at 200 — then skip the cap checks; use `if`.)
4. 'forced Uroboros kill ends the game with the right winner' — the probe-verified scenario: build a state with Uroboros ON TOP of the mansion and give seat 0 the Rocket Launcher + ammo in hand, then run the loop and assert the game ends by uroborosDefeated in ONE action with winner seat 0:
   ```ts
   const s = setup({ seed: 1, players: ['hard', 'hard', 'hard', 'hard'].map((_, i) => ({ name: 'Bot' + i, controller: 'bot' as const })) }, PREMIER_CARDS, FIRST_TIMER_PILES);
   const forced: GameState = {
     ...s,
     mansionDeck: ['ma-010#1'],
     cards: { ...s.cards,
       'we-008#1': { instanceId: 'we-008#1', cardId: 'we-008', location: { zone: 'hand' as const, index: 0 } },
       'am-001#1': { instanceId: 'am-001#1', cardId: 'am-001', location: { zone: 'hand' as const, index: 0 } },
     },
     seats: s.seats.map((p, i) => (i === 0 ? { ...p, hand: ['we-008#1', 'am-001#1'], turnAmmo: 0, turnGold: 0 } : p)),
   };
   const r = playBotGameFrom(forced, ['hard', 'hard', 'hard', 'hard'], () => 0.5, 10);
   ```
   Asserts: r.ended === 'gameOver'; r.actionsApplied === 1 (probe-verified: the hard bot explores with the Rocket Launcher on step 0 and wins — Uroboros has 90 HP, the launcher has instantKillAtOrBelowHp 90 plus damage 100); checkWin(r.state).winners deep-equals [0] (seat 0 defeated it); checkWin(r.state).endReason === 'uroborosDefeated'. (Episode replay: the mansion top is ma-010#1, so exploring reveals Uroboros directly.)
5. 'all-dead end can be forced too' — seat all 4 players dead, then a single endTurn passes through startTurn and checkWin flags allPlayersDead:
   ```ts
   const s = setup({ seed: 1, players: ['hard', 'hard', 'hard', 'hard'].map((_, i) => ({ name: 'Bot' + i, controller: 'bot' as const })) }, PREMIER_CARDS, FIRST_TIMER_PILES);
   const dead = { ...s, seats: s.seats.map(p => ({ ...p, dead: true })) } as GameState;
   const r = playBotGameFrom(dead, ['hard', 'hard', 'hard', 'hard'], () => 0.5, 3);
   ```
   Asserts: r.ended === 'gameOver'; checkWin(r.state).gameOver === true; checkWin(r.state).endReason === 'allPlayersDead'; checkWin(r.state).winners deep-equals [].
   (Behavior verified: with all seats dead, chooseBotAction falls back to endTurn for seat 0, applyAction endTurn succeeds, startTurn runs, and checkWin sees every seat dead.)
6. 'twenty-game soak at a glance' — run 20 games total with the plan mix and REQUIRE at least one 'gameOver' across them (the forced-win scenarios above count separately, so add 4 natural games with a LARGE cap to give the ending a real chance): for seed = 1..4: playBotGame({ seed, difficulties: ['easy', 'easy', 'normal', 'hard'], maxActions: 3000 }); collect ended flags. THEN assert: every game ended ('gameOver' or 'cap') and `gameOverCount >= 0` is trivially true — the REAL assertion is `errors === 0` (no throw). Count nothing else. Print nothing. Just: `expect(true).toBe(true);` after the loop — the test's value is that 4 × 3000-action full games with real data run without throwing. (Do not assert a minimum gameOver count for NATURAL games — the greedy policy does not reliably stack 90 damage; that is known and fine.)

Do NOT add or reorder tests; do not add exports beyond the two functions + two interfaces. The fixture for tests 4/5 uses exact instance ids used by the real data (we-008#1, am-001#1, ma-010#1) — these exist in the registry additions or the real mansion. Trust the verified expected values.
